unit AMQP.ServerRecoveryTests;

{$mode delphi}{$H+}

{ GENERATED FILE — produced by tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py from
  tests/Server/AMQP.ServerRecoveryTests.pas (DUnitX). Do not edit by hand: edit the DUnitX
  master and run the script again. }

{ WAL replay -- WS6 of Phase 4 (durability, see CLAUDE.md, D19-D28).

  The question these tests ask is a single one: GIVEN THIS LOG, WHAT IS THE
  STATE? None of them brings up a broker, socket or thread -- the log is written
  right here, record by record, and read back. That is what allows pinning down
  the replay's semantics instead of just noting that "it recovered something".

  WHAT HAS TO BE PROVED, AND WHY IT IS NOT OBVIOUS

  1. Deleting a queue deletes what was PENDING in it. An implementation that only
     took the queue off the list would pass any topology test -- and would resurrect
     messages of a queue that no longer exists.

  2. Deleting an exchange takes the bindings on BOTH sides. An exchange can be
     the source of some and the destination of others (D5): looking only at Source
     leaves an orphan binding pointing at what vanished.

  3. The order of the placements is the order of the LOG. It is what makes D27
     come for free: the priority bucket is inside the ENQ, so re-enqueueing in
     reading order puts each message where D12's RequeueFront would put it.

  4. ONE content for N placements (D22), and content nobody references is
     RELEASED. Without that, recovery would load into memory the body of every
     message the broker has ever seen -- the log is append-only.

  5. The highest id seen survives. If the engine's counter restarted from zero,
     a new placement would collide with a recovered one, and the DEQ of one
     would retire the other. Silent and fatal.

  6. A broken tail replays the PREFIX (D23). It is the NORMAL case after a crash,
     not an error.

  The three Check* functions exist so the test bodies are IDENTICAL in the
  two dialects: only their implementation changes sides.

  DUnitX master in the FPCUnit dialect (TAssert.*); the FPCUnit mirror in
  fpc\ is generated by tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py. }


